{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790577111000-vm-engineer-remote-core-correlation.prompt.md'","aggregated_output":"# Dispatch: one-envelope migration slice 2 — remote correlation integrity + pair channel loss\n\nRole: dao.stream engineer. Implement migration slice 2 of the\none-envelope ruling\n(collab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md\n— read its \"Migration order\" item 2 and the \"Identity\" section).\nThese are the two independent remote-core fixes the ruling ordered\nBEFORE the wire-vocabulary commit. Fable's final sign-off flagged\nboth (P3 forgeable answer, P2-2 never-ending pair).\n\n## Change 1: absorb! identity check (src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:401-438)\n\nToday absorb! accepts any well-formed answer whose id is outstanding\n-- it looks up ONLY by id (:dao.stream.remote/id v). The mirror\nechoes the request identity, so a well-formed answer also carries\n:dao.stream/identity. A hostile or buggy writer who can append to a\nreader can currently complete someone else's request.\n\nRuled behavior: an answer is accepted only when BOTH the correlation\nid AND the :dao.stream/identity match the outstanding entry's\nreflection identity. The check must run BEFORE any state change --\ntoday the (swap! link update :outstanding dissoc id) and\n(swap! refl update :ids disj id) at :413-414 happen before the cond;\nthe identity gate must precede them. A mismatched answer is a\ndiagnostic only: the request stays outstanding, the reflection's id\nset is untouched, nothing is filed, nothing emitted, no error\nraised. Find where the reflection's own identity lives (the refl\nrecord / its descriptor) and compare against it.\n\n## Change 2: pair reader ends on in-stream not-found/channel-gone\n(src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:94-110)\n\nThe pair reader's 'next' translates only :dao.stream/gap and\n:dao.stream/end from the in stream into :dao.stream/end. A reclaimed\nrelay pair makes the in reflection answer :dao.stream/transport-error\nwith :dao.stream.remote/reason :dao.stream.remote/not-found (or\nchannel-gone); that falls through the default branch raw, the outer\nlink's drain! (remote.cljc:443+) ignores every outcome except\nok/blocked/end/gap, channel-loss! never runs, outstanding appends\nnever get append-unknown, and inner cursor/next keep answering\nretry?/blocked forever.\n\nRuled behavior: on in-stream :dao.stream/transport-error whose\nreason is not-found or channel-gone, end the pair binding exactly as\ngap/end do (reset! ended? true, return :dao.stream/end) so the outer\nlink observes the loss and abandons outstanding appends with\nappend-unknown. Do not change gap/end behavior; do not end on other\ntransport errors (retryable ones keep their current pass-through).\n\n## Test pins (test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc, test/dao/stream/remote_pair_test.cljc)\n\n1. A wrong-identity answer (correct id, different identity) is\n   dropped as a diagnostic: the request REMAINS outstanding, and the\n   correct answer arriving afterwards still completes it normally.\n2. A pair whose in stream answers not-found (e.g. the relay pair was\n   reclaimed) ends the binding: the outer link's drain! runs\n   channel-loss! -- outstanding appends observe append-unknown -- and\n   no waiter is left polling forever. (There is an existing gap-path\n   pin to model the assertion shape on.)\n3. Retryable transport errors on in do NOT end the binding\n   (regression guard for the pass-through).\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Allowed files: src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc,\n  src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc,\n  test/dao/stream/remote_test.cljc,\n  test/dao/stream/remote_pair_test.cljc ONLY.\n- Run via mise from repo root: focused namespaces first\n  (mise exec -- clojure -M:test -n dao.stream.remote-test etc.),\n  then the full JVM suite. All green before reporting (the known\n  yin.repl.main-test intermittent is the ONE tolerated failure if it\n  appears; note it explicitly).\n- cljstyle check on touched files.\n- Do NOT git add or commit -- the orchestrator stages and commits\n  after the gate.\n- Report to collab/1790577111000-vm-engineer-remote-core-correlation.glm-flash.report.md:\n  changes with line refs, exact test commands + counts, git status\n  output.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/1790575143000-architect-one-envelope-ruling.gpt-6-sol.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-09-28 06:03:19 GMT  \nCompleted-Local: 2026-09-28 13:03:19 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\n\n## Ruling\n\nConverge **the application wire vocabulary** on `dao.stream.apply`. Keep `dao.stream.apply` as the open envelope contract and `dao.stream.rpc` as the client driver. This is a small breaking change while the rpc vocabulary is newly landed and gated; a compatibility shim would leave two accepted wire languages and prolong the drift this ruling is meant to end.\n\nThere are two corrections to the proposed direction. First, the present rpc client has random safe ID allocation and tracks outstanding requests, but it has **no numeric cap on the outstanding map**: `request!` allocates whenever there is no retained unsent request, and acceptance adds an entry to `:outstanding` without a size check ([rpc.cljc:212](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:212), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233), [rpc.cljc:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:254), [rpc.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:301)). Add an explicit, caller supplied positive outstanding limit, with a documented finite default. At the limit, return a local backpressure outcome without allocating an ID or appending a request. Second, “plain apply envelopes” describes the **values on the request and response streams**; the lower `dao.stream.remote` protocol still wraps stream operations in its own id, identity, and outcome envelope ([remote.cljc:48](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:48), [remote.cljc:138](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:138)). It must carry the apply value verbatim.\n\n## Target contract\n\n| Concern | Ruling |\n|---|---|\n| Request | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/op keyword :dao.stream.apply/args vector}`, with arbitrary additional keys. Use `apply/request?` on received values; preserve the entire map when forwarding or retaining it. This is the existing open contract ([apply.cljc:31](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:31), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38)). |\n| Success | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/ok value}`; `nil` is a valid value ([apply.cljc:77](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:77), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\n| Error | `{:dao.stream.apply/id id :dao.stream.apply/error {:dao.stream.apply/code qualified-keyword :dao.stream.apply/message string}}`, with additional envelope or error-map keys permitted ([apply.cljc:63](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:63), [apply.cljc:83](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:83), [apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). |\n| Validation | rpc’s former `answer?` checks only safe ID and exclusive presence of `ok` or `error`; it does **not** validate the error body. The apply predicate does validate its qualified code and string message ([rpc.cljc:111](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:111), [apply.cljc:70](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:70)). Use `apply/response?` for decoding, then apply rpc’s safe-ID check at its client boundary. Malformed answers become rpc diagnostics and are consumed once, as its polling path already does ([rpc.cljc:358](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:358), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). |\n| Diagnostics and completions | Remain rpc-local events and state, outside the apply wire envelope. They record unsolicited or malformed responses, gaps, terminal loss, and completed work ([rpc.cljc:29](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:29), [rpc.cljc:192](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:192), [rpc.cljc:385](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:385), [rpc.cljc:486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:486)). Their public output keys may stay rpc-namespaced; the embedded request and response values must be apply envelopes. |\n\nThe field mapping is lossless for application requests and answers: rpc `id/op/args`, `id/ok`, and `id/error/code/message` each have an apply counterpart ([rpc.cljc:71](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:71), [rpc.cljc:99](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:99), [apply.cljc:15](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:15)). The stricter ID domain and the event machinery are client policy, not missing application fields.\n\n**IDs.** Leave `apply/correlation-id?` as `some?`: the contract deliberately accepts opaque non-nil IDs and assigns cross-host allocation policy to rpc ([apply.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:24)). rpc continues to mint random integers in its cross-host safe range, reroll collisions against IDs still in use, and fail explicitly after its retry bound ([rpc.cljc:24](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:24), [rpc.cljc:35](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:35), [rpc.cljc:60](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:60), [rpc.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:144), [rpc.cljc:233](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:233)). Document and enforce `safe-id?` and the new outstanding cap **in rpc**, including on received answers. Do not reject another valid apply producer’s opaque ID at the apply layer.\n\n**Errors.** Keep transport or client failures as rpc completion reasons unless a server is actually sending an application error response. Define distinct, stable apply-qualified reason words for `not-found`, `detached`, `ended`, `no-surface`, `oversize`, and `transport-error`. Map remote `no-surface` to `:dao.stream.apply/no-surface` and remote `oversize` to `:dao.stream.apply/oversize`; do not collapse them into the generic transport reason. `no-surface`, `oversize`, `not-found`, and `ended` are terminal for that binding; `detached` alone permits rebind; a generic transport failure follows the existing terminal path. The current mapping distinguishes only `not-found` and `channel-gone`, while `rebind` already accepts only `detached` ([rpc.cljc:432](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:432), [rpc.cljc:445](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:445), [rpc.cljc:568](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/rpc.cljc:568)). The remote source already emits separate `no-surface` and `oversize` protocol errors ([remote.cljc:39](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:39), [remote.cljc:123](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:123), [remote.cljc:164](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:164)).\n\n**Identity.** An apply response requires its correlation ID, not a stream identity. Keep it that way: an application envelope can be used over a local pair with no remote identity ([apply.cljc:89](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:89)). A *remote protocol answer* already names `:dao.stream/identity`; its reflection must accept it only when **both** the ID and identity equal those of the outstanding request. Enforce that in `remote/absorb!` before removing the outstanding entry, marking a reflection gone, filing an answer, installing `more`, or emitting an append outcome. A mismatch is a diagnostic, leaving the legitimate request outstanding. The mirror echoes the request identity, but `absorb!` currently looks up only by ID ([remote.cljc:82](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:82), [remote.cljc:401](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:401)). This is correlation integrity within the remote protocol, not authentication of whoever can write to the channel.\n\n## Serving and end-to-end acceptance\n\nThe serving composition creates the call-in and call-out media, mints their cursors from their actual handles, registers each handle and declared surface in its remote mirror table, and retains that table, channel, cursor, and any lease for as long as the call can be resumed. The remote mirror serves stream operations; the FFI interpreter reads apply requests and appends apply responses. `apply/serve-once!` already takes caller-owned cursor state, retains a computed response across `full`, and never mints a cursor itself ([apply.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:217), [apply.cljc:301](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:301)). The mirror likewise holds only its returned channel cursor and forwards the served stream’s cursors ([remote.cljc:185](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:185)).\n\nSpecify the UCF `serve!` callback as **idempotent per live handle within one export binding**: repeated calls return the same identity and channel and refer to the same table entry; retirement or lease reclaim ends that binding explicitly. `lift-frame` can call `serve!` while minting cells and again while lifting entries, so unstable markers would break a single frame ([ucf/remote.cljc:217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:217), [ucf/remote.cljc:435](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:435)). Implement a production composition that owns this registry and its lease lifecycle; the UCF facade only accepts the callback and makes remote descriptors from it ([ucf/remote.cljc:37](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:37), [ucf/remote.cljc:144](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:144)). A refusal returns `nil` before publishing a lift, as the facade specifies ([ucf/remote.cljc:41](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:41)).\n\n“VM FFI call answered remotely, end-to-end” is accepted only when a real VM call emits its original apply request; the remote serving composition exposes its call-out request stream to an rpc-driven responder; that responder reads the **unchanged** request, appends a valid apply success or error to the response stream; and the VM resumes or raises the corresponding error through `yin.vm.ffi/call-result`, with the same ID and any extra request keys preserved where the request is retained or transported. Verify pending append retries, response `full`, gap, detach and rebind, terminal not-found, and UCF lift/lower of a retained call. The VM already constructs apply requests and checks apply response correlation ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [ffi.cljc:88](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ffi.cljc:88)); UCF already carries a retained request envelope verbatim ([ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\n\n## Migration order\n\n1. **Contract and driver, one breaking commit.** Replace rpc request and answer constructors, predicates, and accessors with apply equivalents at the wire boundary. Preserve rpc’s client state machine, event names, completion API, ID allocator, and cursor minting. Add the outstanding cap and the distinct error reasons. Migrate rpc tests and the REPL adapter/server tests together. A request emitted by `rpc/request!` and an answer accepted by `rpc/poll!` must pass the apply predicates; rpc must reject an invalid error body and diagnose an unsafe reply ID. Current wire consumers include `yin.repl.adapter` and `yin.repl.serve` ([adapter.cljc:50](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:50), [adapter.cljc:169](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/adapter.cljc:169), [serve.cljc:424](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:424), [serve.cljc:503](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/serve.cljc:503)). `yin.repl.connect` creates and rebinds the driver, and `yin.repl.driver` reads its completion IDs; update their assertions where affected ([connect.cljc:422](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:422), [connect.cljc:516](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/connect.cljc:516), [driver.cljc:383](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/repl/driver.cljc:383)). The polling observation layer documents rpc and apply as separate state transitions, not separate wire contracts ([observe.cljc:28](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/observe.cljc:28)).\n2. **Remote correlation and channel loss.** Add the identity check in `remote/absorb!`. Make a pair reader’s in-stream `not-found` or `channel-gone` end that pair binding and propagate loss to the outer link, as it already does for gap and end ([remote_pair.cljc:58](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:58), [remote_pair.cljc:95](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote_pair.cljc:95), [remote.cljc:443](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/remote.cljc:443)). Acceptance: a wrong-identity answer cannot complete a request, and a dead pair cannot leave waiters polling forever.\n3. **Production FFI serving composition.** Supply the stable per-handle `serve!` registry, cursor and reflection ownership, lease retirement, and a remote responder using apply values. Demonstrate the end-to-end VM case above, including UCF retained-envelope round trip.\n\nThe correlation check and pair termination are independent remote-core fixes and may land before slice 1. The `no-surface`/`oversize` mapping and outstanding cap belong to convergence’s driver acceptance. Stable `serve!`, leased ownership, and the end-to-end case are required before claiming **remote FFI** complete; they need not block the wire-vocabulary commit.\n\nDo **not** rename yin’s `dao.stream.apply/call` syntax or AST: that name is parsed and used throughout the VM ([yin.vm.cljc:194](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:194), [semantic.cljc:552](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:552)). Do **not** rebuild VM requests from op and args, tighten apply’s open predicates to rpc’s allocation domain, or add rpc event fields to the application envelope. The VM already emits apply requests, the apply protocol admits additional keys, and UCF explicitly retains the whole envelope because rebuilding it loses those keys ([semantic.cljc:467](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc:467), [apply.cljc:38](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/dao/stream/apply.cljc:38), [ucf/remote.cljc:328](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/remote.cljc:328)).\n\nVerdict: ADOPTED WITH CHANGES","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\n\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\n\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\n\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\nto the host and cannot invoke application code. The adapter is handed only the\ndeposit operation and its own host resource. If its deposit channel fails, it\nmay close that resource to make the boundary observably gone; this is teardown,\nnot an upward callback.\n\nHost types are classified into plain data in the transform: it is the last\nplace holding a host error object, and no host type crosses onto the stream. A\ntransport may use a qualified envelope to preserve correlation, lifecycle, and\nadditive evolution; that envelope remains plain data and creates no separate\nevent mechanism. A composition may likewise define its own qualified vocabulary\nfor facts it owns.\n\nWhich stream an adapter writes to is the caller's composition, never the\nadapter's knowledge. An adapter cannot tell whether it deposits into one\ntransport's private stream or into a medium many interpreters read. This keeps\nthe layering one-way: a boundary depends on nothing above it, because whoever\nconstructs the adapter supplies the stream.\n\nA transform deposits every event admitted by the boundary protocol and judges\nnone of them semantically. Wire framing, decoding, and protocol validation\nhappen below the transform: malformed wire input is transport machinery, not an\nevent. Once the transport has produced a valid host event, data is syntax\n(axiom 2): an event that is noise to one interpreter is signal to another that\ndoes not exist yet, and filtering at the depositing layer destroys perspectives\nnot yet taken. Retention is the medium's operational policy; meaning is\ninterpretation. Neither is the depositor's decision.\n\nTwo things are not adapters. One that exposes a function for portable code to\ncall isolates the host library but keeps the coupling, and the effect never\nappears as an emission. One that takes a function to invoke has relocated the\ncallback, not removed it, even when every value it passes is plain data; if\nthat callback has a meaningful return value, it is a synchronous call in both\ndirections wearing the shape of an event.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
